WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.600
A Japanese form of 19 July 1916: "2429 1378" reads 文密, followed by the enciphered letters.

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:07.000
The message is a stream of letters, cut into ten-letter words.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:11.500
Read it two letters at a time: each syllable stands for a number from 00 to 99.

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:16.000
The G0 grid gives that number: the consonant gives the tens, the vowel the units.

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:19.500
Two syllables make a four-digit Chinese telegraph code, hence one character.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:25.500
And so on, with no other key.

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:29.400
"A cancellation is reported; the attitude of the Japanese has suddenly changed…"

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:35.500
The last code makes no sense: a single letter is wrong (i instead of e, ·· instead of · in Morse). Corrected: 皓, "the 19th".

